WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.023
What does a nerve signal look like, and what makes it?

2
00:00:06.967 --> 00:00:12.127
Here is the membrane potential of a model squid giant axon, over a few milliseconds.

3
00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:14.610
The trace starts flat.

4
00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:18.310
This is rest, at about minus −65 mV.

5
00:00:19.183 --> 00:00:22.343
Now we nudge it. A small nudge dies away.

6
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:24.283
So does another.

7
00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:26.967
A slightly bigger nudge crosses a line.

8
00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:29.827
For this stimulus, that line is threshold.

9
00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:33.127
Past it, the voltage keeps rising on its own.

10
00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:36.567
It shoots up in a fraction of a millisecond.

11
00:00:37.207 --> 00:00:38.327
This is the rising phase.

12
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.380
It crosses zero and keeps going.

13
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.840
At the peak the inside is positive, about plus +40 mV.

14
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.260
Going past zero is the overshoot.

15
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:52.190
Then the falling phase brings it back down, past rest.

16
00:00:53.183 --> 00:00:55.483
It dips below rest for several milliseconds.

17
00:00:56.503 --> 00:00:57.603
That is the undershoot.

18
00:00:58.443 --> 00:00:59.443
Then it settles back.

19
00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:02.967
The whole event is an action potential, or spike.

20
00:01:03.847 --> 00:01:05.027
It lasts a few milliseconds.

21
00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:12.527
In 1939 Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley recorded this from inside a squid giant axon.

22
00:01:13.307 --> 00:01:14.527
The overshoot was a surprise.

23
00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:18.517
The leading theory said the voltage should stop near zero.

24
00:01:19.497 --> 00:01:20.497
It did not.

25
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.420
A question to hold.

26
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.500
If we push much harder, will the spike be bigger?

27
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:35.690
No. It comes sooner, but it is essentially the same size.

28
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:39.150
With brief pushes from rest, the height barely changes.

29
00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:41.150
This is all-or-none.

30
00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:43.803
Now two pushes close together.

31
00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:47.323
The first fires. The second fails.

32
00:01:47.663 --> 00:01:49.603
This is refractoriness.

33
00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:51.613
So we have puzzles.

34
00:01:52.753 --> 00:01:53.753
Why the overshoot?

35
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:56.833
Why a threshold? Why all-or-none?

36
00:01:57.673 --> 00:01:58.773
Why refractoriness?

37
00:01:59.817 --> 00:02:03.497
And why does the spike travel along the axon without turning back?

38
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.100
One rule, three gates and a little cable theory answer all five.

39
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.480
A spike is all or none.

40
00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:12.240
It has a threshold.

41
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.940
And it needs a rest before the next one.
